### What is the Shortest Common Supersequence?

A common supersequence of two strings is a string that contains both as subsequences. The shortest common supersequence (SCS) is the shortest such string. For example, for `"ABAC"` and `"CAB"`, one SCS is `"CABAC"` with length 5.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// A = "ABAC", B = "CAB"
// SCS: "CABAC" (length 5)
// Check: "ABAC" is a subsequence of "CABAC"? A(1),B(2),A(3),C(4) -> yes
// Check: "CAB" is a subsequence of "CABAC"? C(0),A(1),B(2) -> yes
```

### Why does SCS length equal len(A) + len(B) - LCS?

A supersequence must include every character of both strings in their original order. The only characters that can overlap between the two strings and be written a single time are those forming a common subsequence. To minimize the length, we maximize the overlap, which is the longest common subsequence. Every other character is unique to one string and must appear separately. So SCS = len(A) + len(B) - LCS.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// A = "ABAC" (length 4), B = "CAB" (length 3)
// LCS of A and B: "AB" (length 2)
// SCS length = 4 + 3 - 2 = 5
// The LCS characters "AB" are shared, written once
// Remaining: "AC" from A and "C" from B are unique
```

### How do you compute the SCS length?

First compute the LCS using the standard 2D DP table, then apply the formula. The LCS table is the one where match gives diagonal+1 and mismatch gives max(top, left).

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ShortestCommonSupersequence {

    public int scsLength(String a, String b) {
        int lcsLen = lcs(a, b);
        return a.length() + b.length() - lcsLen;
    }

    private int lcs(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[m][n];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ShortestCommonSupersequence solver = new ShortestCommonSupersequence();
        System.out.println(solver.scsLength("ABAC", "CAB"));
        System.out.println(solver.scsLength("TRANSFER", "TRANSFORM"));
    }
}
```

```text
5
10
```

### How do you reconstruct the actual SCS string?

Walk the LCS DP table from dp[m][n] back to dp[0][0]. When the characters match, emit the character once and move diagonally. When they differ, emit the character from the side with the larger DP value and move toward it. After reaching the border, emit any remaining characters from the unfinished string.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SCSReconstructor {

    public String findSCS(String a, String b) {
        int m = a.length();
        int n = b.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[m + 1][n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1;
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        int i = m, j = n;
        while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
            if (a.charAt(i - 1) == b.charAt(j - 1)) {
                sb.append(a.charAt(i - 1));
                i--;
                j--;
            } else if (dp[i - 1][j] > dp[i][j - 1]) {
                sb.append(a.charAt(i - 1));
                i--;
            } else {
                sb.append(b.charAt(j - 1));
                j--;
            }
        }
        while (i > 0) { sb.append(a.charAt(i - 1)); i--; }
        while (j > 0) { sb.append(b.charAt(j - 1)); j--; }

        return sb.reverse().toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SCSReconstructor solver = new SCSReconstructor();
        System.out.println(solver.findSCS("ABAC", "CAB"));
    }
}
```

```text
CABAC
```

### How is SCS related to LCS and Edit Distance?

SCS, LCS, and Edit Distance are all members of the Longest Common Substring pattern family. They share the same 2D DP table structure but answer different questions about the relationship between two strings.

| Problem | Formula/Answer | Uses LCS table |
|---|---|---|
| LCS length | dp[m][n] | Directly |
| SCS length | m + n - LCS | Subtract LCS from total |
| Edit distance (insert/delete) | m + n - 2 * LCS | Two-way complement |
| Edit distance (with substitution) | Own recurrence | Related but separate |

```java
package com.bank.core;

// All three use the same 2D DP table structure
// LCS: match -> diagonal+1, mismatch -> max(top,left)
// SCS: len(A) + len(B) - LCS
// Edit distance (insert/delete only): len(A) + len(B) - 2*LCS
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include: one string is empty (SCS is the other string), both strings are identical (SCS is the string itself), one string is a subsequence of the other (SCS is the longer string), and strings with no common characters (SCS is their concatenation).

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Edge case 1: A="", B="ABC" -> SCS = "ABC" (len=3)
// Edge case 2: A=B="ABC" -> LCS=3, SCS=3+3-3=3 ("ABC")
// Edge case 3: A="AC", B="ABC" -> A is subsequence of B, SCS = B (len=3)
// Edge case 4: A="XY", B="AB" -> no common chars, SCS = "XYAB" or "ABXY" (len=4)
```

### What about a banking use case?

At the National Bank of India, two branch servers capture partial transaction logs. The compliance team merges them into one combined log that preserves both orderings while writing shared events only once.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class LogMerger {

    private final SCSReconstructor solver = new SCSReconstructor();

    public String mergeLogs(String log1, String log2) {
        String merged = solver.findSCS(log1, log2);
        System.out.println("Log 1: " + log1);
        System.out.println("Log 2: " + log2);
        System.out.println("Merged: " + merged + " (length " + merged.length() + ")");
        return merged;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LogMerger merger = new LogMerger();
        merger.mergeLogs("DWTD", "DWSD");
    }
}
```

```text
Log 1: DWTD
Log 2: DWSD
Merged: DWSTD (length 5)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to handle the remaining characters after one pointer reaches zero during reconstruction. Another mistake is confusing SCS with LCS and using the wrong formula.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// WRONG: forgetting remaining characters
while (i > 0 && j > 0) { /* ... */ }
// Missing: while (i > 0) append a[i-1]
// Missing: while (j > 0) append b[j-1]

// CORRECT: handle remaining after main loop
while (i > 0) { sb.append(a.charAt(i-1)); i--; }
while (j > 0) { sb.append(b.charAt(j-1)); j--; }
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that SCS is the natural complement of LCS, and computing one immediately gives the other. The reconstruction walk is the same as LCS reconstruction but emits characters from both sides instead of only matching characters. For length-only computation, the rolling-row optimization reduces space to O(min(m, n)), but reconstruction requires the full table.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Senior notes:
// 1. SCS = complement of LCS: same table, different interpretation
// 2. Reconstruction: emit from both sides, not just matches
// 3. Space optimization: O(min(m,n)) for length, O(m*n) for reconstruction
// 4. Time: O(m*n) dominated by LCS computation
// 5. Practical use: merge sorted logs, combine partial sequences
```
